<h1>how to get into IT with no experience</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">Start at helpdesk, build a homelab, and document everything you fix. The IT industry runs on two currencies: vendor certs that get you past HR filters, and proof that you can troubleshoot under pressure. Most people collect the first and skip the second, which is why they stay stuck on the phones.</p>

<div class="tldr">
<ul>
<li>Helpdesk is the honest entry point: roughly two-thirds of sysadmins and network engineers started there, and the role teaches you the business context that cert courses ignore.</li>
<li>CompTIA A+ and Network+ are real screening keys for IT support roles, but a documented homelab, a GitHub of scripts, or a ticket-style writeup of a problem you solved beats a cert alone when a technical hiring manager actually reviews your application.</li>
<li>The helpdesk-to-sysadmin ladder is not automatic: it requires you to volunteer for infrastructure work, automate the boring parts of your current job, and make your next employer's needs visible before you need the title.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.naceweb.org/talent-acquisition/internships/intern-conversion-rate-hits-highest-mark-in-five-years">Internship-to-full-time conversion in structured programs hit 63.1% in 2024-25</a>, the highest in five years, meaning a paid IT internship is the fastest no-experience path if you can land one.</li>
<li>Recruiters in IT increasingly use skills-based screening: <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers now use skills-based hiring in 2025</a>, up from 81%, which means a scored troubleshooting simulation or a documented project can bypass the "two years experience required" wall entirely.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<section>
<h2>Why helpdesk is the real starting line, not a trap</h2>

<p>The IT industry loves to warn people away from helpdesk. "You'll be resetting passwords forever," they say. What they do not say is that most infrastructure engineers, security analysts, and cloud architects spent their first 12-24 months on a service desk, and the ones who escaped fastest were not the ones with the most certs. They were the ones who treated the role as an intelligence-gathering operation.</p>

<p>Helpdesk is where you learn the organization's actual pain. You see which tickets repeat, which systems fail under load, which vendor promises are lies. You learn to translate user panic into technical diagnosis, which is the core skill of every senior IT role. You also get access: access to the network diagram nobody updates, the server room you are not supposed to touch, the senior engineer who hates documentation but knows where every body is buried.</p>

<p>The escape velocity depends on what you do with that access. The people who stay on phones for five years treat tickets as interruptions. The people who leave in 18 months treat tickets as data. They build a private wiki of solutions. They script the password resets. They ask to shadow the network upgrade. They make their manager's life easier until their manager fights to keep them from leaving for another team.</p>

<p>This is the filter that separates IT careerists from IT tourists, where the cert gets you the interview and the curiosity gets you the promotion.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Where certs matter and where they are wallpaper</h2>

<p>CompTIA A+ is a genuine screening key for entry-level IT support roles, especially at managed service providers, government contractors, and large enterprises with rigid HR processes. It is not a knowledge test so much as a signal that you can stick with a boring curriculum and pass a standardized exam, which correlates tolerably well with "can follow a troubleshooting procedure without improvising dangerous shortcuts." Network+ and Security+ function similarly: they get you past the algorithm, not the technical interview.</p>

<p>The problem is that certs stop working as you climb. A sysadmin candidate with A+ and no homelab looks like a person who buys credentials instead of building skills. A candidate with no certs but a documented migration from pfSense to OPNsense, a writeup of diagnosing a DNS loop, and a Python script that parses syslog for anomalies looks like someone who learns by breaking and fixing things, which is what infrastructure roles actually require.</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Role stage</th>
<th>What gets you the interview</th>
<th>What gets you the offer</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Helpdesk / L1 support</td>
<td>A+, customer service background, any tech-adjacent job</td>
<td>Ticket documentation, calm under simulated user stress, basic networking logic</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Desktop / field tech</td>
<td>A+, Network+, hardware familiarity</td>
<td>Imaging and deployment stories, driver troubleshooting evidence, user communication samples</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Sysadmin / infrastructure</td>
<td>Network+, Security+, cloud associate certs (AWS/Azure)</td>
<td>Homelab architecture, automation scripts, incident response writeups, migration documentation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Security / cloud / SRE</td>
<td>Security+, CISSP (associate), cloud professional certs</td>
<td>CTF writeups, detection rule development, cost optimization case studies, on-call runbooks you wrote</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The honest frame: early IT hiring is credential-heavy because the signal-to-noise ratio is brutal and HR lacks technical judgment. Mid-level and senior hiring shifts to proof because the cost of a bad hire is a production outage. Your career strategy should match the transition: use certs to get in, then replace them with documented work as fast as possible.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>The homelab as your unlisted work history</h2>

<p>IT is uniquely friendly to self-taught proof because the tools are cheap and the failures are private. A software developer needs someone to give them a codebase to contribute to. An IT candidate can build an entire fake enterprise network in VirtualBox or Proxmox, break it deliberately, and document the fix.</p>

<p>The minimum viable homelab for a helpdesk-to-sysadmin candidate: a hypervisor running a Windows domain controller, a Linux file server, a pfSense or OPNsense router, and a few client VMs. Configure DHCP, DNS, and Active Directory. Break DNS and fix it. Set up a VPN. Document everything in a format that mimics an internal knowledge base: problem statement, environment, steps taken, resolution, what you would do differently.</p>

<p>The next level: automate the boring parts. A PowerShell or Python script that creates users from a CSV. A Terraform configuration that spins up the lab in one command. A monitoring stack with Prometheus and Grafana that alerts when a VM dies. These are not "projects" in the software sense. They are decision records with consequences, and they demonstrate the exact judgment that IT hiring managers are guessing at when they read your resume.</p>

<p>The presentation matters. A GitHub repo with a README is fine. A Notion or Obsidian site with searchable writeups is better. What wins is the story: "I noticed my DNS resolution was slow, suspected a loop, used Wireshark to confirm, reconfigured the forwarder, and documented the fix so the next person does not spend two hours on it." That is the skill helpdesk teaches and sysadmin requires.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>The ladder: how to move from password resets to production systems</h2>

<p>The helpdesk-to-sysadmin path is not a title change handed down by a generous manager. It is a series of lateral moves you engineer by making your next role visible before it exists. The pattern is consistent across the people who actually make the jump in 18-36 months instead of never.</p>

<p>First six months: master the scope of your current role and volunteer for everything adjacent. Backup restores, printer network moves, software deployments, anything that touches infrastructure even briefly. Document what you did, what went wrong, and what you learned. This builds your internal reputation and your external portfolio simultaneously.</p>

<p>Months six to twelve: automate or improve a process that annoys your team. A script that clears stale AD accounts. A checklist that reduces onboarding tickets. A monitoring alert that catches disk space issues before they become emergencies. The goal is not the tool; it is the conversation with your manager where you say, "I noticed we spend four hours a week on X, so I built Y, and here is the time saved." That conversation is the audition for the next role.</p>

<p>Months twelve to twenty-four: identify the team you want to join and start doing their work before you ask for the transfer. Shadow their on-call rotation. Pick up their documentation debt. Fix a small issue in their queue during a slow week. When a junior opening appears, you are not a helpdesk person applying. You are a provisional member who needs the title to match the work.</p>

<p>If internal movement is blocked, the same portfolio gets you external offers. The market for IT generalists with proof of work is tighter than the market for cert collectors, but the salaries are 30-50% higher because the risk to the employer is lower.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Where zero fits in the IT path</h2>

<p>zero does not replace the CompTIA A+ for getting past HR at a government contractor. It replaces the gap between "I passed a test" and "I can handle a production incident." The platform runs company-shaped IT scenarios: a simulated service desk queue where your ticket responses are scored on accuracy and tone, a network troubleshooting simulation where you diagnose a downed VLAN, a security incident where you triage alerts and write a timeline.</p>

<p>The output is scored proof of work, not a certificate. A recruiter sees that you cleared 87% of simulated tier-1 tickets with above-median escalation judgment, not that you completed a 40-hour course. For the 85% of employers now using skills-based hiring, that scored proof is a direct signal. For the remaining 15%, it is still a portfolio piece that wins the technical interview after the cert gets you the screening call.</p>

<p>The honest positioning: if you are starting from zero, combine the paths. Get A+ to satisfy the filter, build the homelab to satisfy the hiring manager, and use scored simulations to close the gap between "I studied" and "I performed under pressure." The old path collects credentials and hopes someone notices, while the new one builds proof and gets matched to roles that actually need the work done.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
<h2>Real questions people ask</h2>

<h3>Do I need a degree to get into IT?</h3>
<p>No for most helpdesk and sysadmin paths, yes for some government and enterprise roles where HR requires any bachelor's as a compliance checkbox. The degree functions as status certainty, not skill verification. If you have one, it helps at large organizations. If you do not, certs plus proof of work route around it at most mid-size and smaller companies.</p>

<h3>How long does it take to go from helpdesk to sysadmin?</h3>
<p>18 to 36 months for people who actively engineer the move, five-plus years for people who wait to be noticed. The difference is documentation, automation, and asking for infrastructure work before you have the title. There is no automatic promotion pipeline in IT. Every ladder is self-built.</p>

<h3>Is the Google IT Support Certificate enough to get hired?</h3>
<p>It is a structured introduction to concepts, not a hiring signal on its own. It helps if you pair it with a homelab and documented troubleshooting. Alone, it is roughly equivalent to A+ in HR screening value and slightly below A+ in technical interview recognition. The content is solid; the credential is common.</p>

<h3>What is the cheapest way to build a homelab?</h3>
<p>A used enterprise desktop with 32GB RAM costs $200-400 and runs VirtualBox or Proxmox for a full simulated network. Cloud free tiers (AWS, Azure) work for specific exercises but teach you less about hardware and networking fundamentals. Start local, document everything, then add cloud resources for specific platform certifications.</p>

<h3>Should I specialize early in cloud, security, or networking?</h3>
<p>Generalize for your first 12-18 months, then specialize based on what you actually enjoy fixing. The generalist foundation prevents the common trap of a cloud-certified candidate who cannot troubleshoot basic connectivity, or a security-focused candidate who has never seen a production incident. Breadth first, then depth where your proof of work is strongest.</p>
</section>

